  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4x-{x}^{2}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(1)=4-2(1)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2+3 \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)+1 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,1).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
